<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-relations">

  <title>Relations</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      <em>Prerequisite:</em> Familiarity with sets and Cartesian products from
      <xref ref="sec-elementary-set-theory"/>.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <objectives>
    <ul>
      <li><p>Define a binary relation using Cartesian products.</p></li>
      <li><p>Represent relations using ordered pairs, tables, diagrams, matrices, and directed graphs.</p></li>
      <li><p>Determine the domain and range of a relation.</p></li>
      <li><p>Test whether a relation is reflexive, irreflexive, symmetric, antisymmetric, asymmetric, or transitive.</p></li>
      <li><p>Recognize equivalence relations and equivalence classes.</p></li>
      <li><p>Understand the connection between equivalence relations and partitions.</p></li>
      <li><p>Recognize partial orders and total orders.</p></li>
      <li><p>Compute inverse relations.</p></li>
      <li><p>Compose relations.</p></li>
      <li><p>Distinguish ordinary relations from functions.</p></li>
    </ul>
  </objectives>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-why-relations-matter">
    <title>Why Relations Matter</title>

    <p>Mathematics often studies how objects are connected.</p>

    <p>
      <ul>
        <li><p>One number being less than another.</p></li>
        <li><p>One integer dividing another.</p></li>
        <li><p>Two numbers having the same remainder after division.</p></li>
        <li><p>Two geometric objects being congruent.</p></li>
        <li><p>One person being connected to another in a network.</p></li>
        <li><p>One vertex being adjacent to another in a graph.</p></li>
        <li><p>One input being associated with an output.</p></li>
      </ul>
    </p>

    <p>
      A <em>relation</em> provides a general mathematical way to describe
      such connections.
    </p>

    <p>
      Relations also provide the formal foundation for functions, equivalence
      classes, orderings, graphs, databases, and many other mathematical
      structures.
    </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-binary-relations">
    <title>Binary Relations</title>

    <definition xml:id="def-binary-relation">
      <title>Binary Relation</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          A <em>binary relation</em> from a set <m>A</m> to a set <m>B</m>
          is a subset
          <md>
            R\subseteq A\times B.
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>
      Since <m>A\times B</m> is a set of ordered pairs, a binary relation is
      simply a collection of selected ordered pairs.
    </p>

    <p>
      If
      <md>
        (a,b)\in R,
      </md>
      we say that <m>a</m> is related to <m>b</m> by <m>R</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      This may also be written
      <md>
        aRb.
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>The notation <m>aRb</m> is read <q><m>a</m> is related to <m>b</m> by <m>R</m>.</q></p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-relations-on-set">
    <title>Relations on a Set</title>

    <p>
      A relation may connect elements of two different sets, but a particularly
      important case occurs when both sets are the same.
    </p>

    <definition xml:id="def-relation-on-set">
      <title>Relation on a Set</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          A relation <m>R</m> is a <em>relation on <m>A</m></em> if
          <md>
            R\subseteq A\times A.
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>Thus every ordered pair in <m>R</m> has both entries in <m>A</m>.</p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-relation-example">
    <title>Example of a Relation</title>

    <p>
      Let
      <md>
        A=\{1,2,3\}.
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      Define
      <md>
        R=\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,3),(3,1)\}.
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      Since every ordered pair belongs to <m>A\times A</m>,
      <md>
        R\subseteq A\times A.
      </md>
      Therefore <m>R</m> is a relation on <m>A</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      We may write
      <md>
        1R1,\qquad 1R2,\qquad 2R3,\qquad 3R1.
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      However,
      <md>
        (2,1)\notin R.
      </md>
      Therefore <m>2</m> is not related to <m>1</m> by <m>R</m>.
    </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-relations-defined-rules">
    <title>Relations Defined by Rules</title>

    <p>
      Relations are often described by a rule rather than by listing every
      ordered pair.
    </p>

    <p>
      For example, define a relation <m>R</m> on <m>\Z</m> by
      <md>
        aRb\Longleftrightarrow a&lt;b.
      </md>
      This means that <m>a</m> is related to <m>b</m> exactly when
      <m>a</m> is less than <m>b</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Another relation on <m>\Z</m> may be defined by
      <md>
        aRb\Longleftrightarrow a-b\text{ is even}.
      </md>
      This relation connects integers having the same parity.
    </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-relation-double-arrow">
    <title>The Double Arrow in Definitions</title>

    <p>
      The notation
      <md>
        \Longleftrightarrow
      </md>
      is a double implication arrow.
    </p>

    <p>It is commonly read <q>if and only if</q> or <q>is equivalent to.</q></p>

    <p>
      Thus,
      <md>
        aRb\Longleftrightarrow a-b\text{ is even}
      </md>
      means that the relation <m>aRb</m> holds exactly when
      <m>a-b</m> is even.
    </p>

    <p>
      Formal biconditional logic is developed in
      <xref ref="sec-elementary-logic"/>.
    </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-relation-ordered-pairs">
    <title>Representing Relations with Ordered Pairs</title>

    <p>
      For finite sets, a relation may be represented directly by listing its
      ordered pairs.
    </p>

    <p>
      For example,
      <md>
        R=\{(1,2),(2,1),(2,3),(3,3)\}.
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      This representation makes the exact members of the relation explicit.
      However, it becomes inconvenient for large or infinite relations.
    </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-relation-arrow-diagrams">
    <title>Arrow Diagrams</title>

    <p>
      A relation from <m>A</m> to <m>B</m> may be represented using an arrow
      diagram.
    </p>

    <p>
      Each element of <m>A</m> is displayed as an input object and each element
      of <m>B</m> as an output object.
    </p>

    <p>
      An arrow is drawn from <m>a</m> to <m>b</m> whenever
      <md>
        (a,b)\in R.
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      Unlike functions, a relation may associate one input with several
      outputs, one output with several inputs, or some elements with no
      partners at all.
    </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-relations-digraphs">
    <title>Relations as Directed Graphs</title>

    <p>A relation on a set <m>A</m> can be represented as a directed graph.</p>

    <p>Each element of <m>A</m> becomes a vertex.</p>

    <p>
      If
      <md>
        aRb,
      </md>
      a directed edge is drawn from <m>a</m> to <m>b</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      If
      <md>
        aRa,
      </md>
      the graph contains a loop from <m>a</m> back to itself.
    </p>

    <p>
      This representation makes properties such as reflexivity and symmetry
      easier to visualize.
    </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-relation-matrices">
    <title>Relations as Matrices</title>

    <p>
      If
      <md>
        A=\{a_1,\ldots,a_m\}
      </md>
      and
      <md>
        B=\{b_1,\ldots,b_n\},
      </md>
      a relation <m>R\subseteq A\times B</m> can be represented by an
      <m>m\times n</m> matrix.
    </p>

    <p>
      The entry in row <m>i</m>, column <m>j</m> is commonly defined by
      <md>
        M_{ij}=
        \begin{cases}
        1, &amp; (a_i,b_j)\in R,\\
        0, &amp; (a_i,b_j)\notin R.
        \end{cases}
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      The symbol <m>M_{ij}</m> is read <q><m>M</m> sub <m>i,j</m></q> and denotes
      the entry of matrix <m>M</m> in row <m>i</m>, column <m>j</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Matrix representations of relations become especially useful in
      discrete mathematics and computer science.
    </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-domain-relation">
    <title>Domain of a Relation</title>

    <definition xml:id="def-domain-relation">
      <title>Domain of a Relation</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          For a relation <m>R\subseteq A\times B</m>, the <em>domain</em>
          of <m>R</m> is the set of elements of <m>A</m> that appear as first
          coordinates:
          <md>
            \operatorname{dom}(R)
            =
            \{a\in A:\exists b\in B,\ (a,b)\in R\}.
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>The notation <m>\operatorname{dom}(R)</m> is read <q>the domain of <m>R</m>.</q></p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-range-relation">
    <title>Range of a Relation</title>

    <definition xml:id="def-range-relation">
      <title>Range of a Relation</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          The <em>range</em> of a relation <m>R\subseteq A\times B</m> is
          the set of elements of <m>B</m> that appear as second coordinates:
          <md>
            \operatorname{ran}(R)
            =
            \{b\in B:\exists a\in A,\ (a,b)\in R\}.
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>
  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-worked-domain-range-relation">
    <title>Worked Example: Domain and Range</title>

    <example xml:id="ex-domain-range-relation">
      <title>Find the Domain and Range</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let
          <md>
            R=\{(1,a),(1,b),(3,b),(4,c)\}.
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <p>The first coordinates are <m>1,3,4</m>.</p>
        <p>The second coordinates are <m>a,b,c</m>.</p>
        <p>
          Therefore,
          <md>
            \operatorname{dom}(R)=\{1,3,4\},
            \qquad
            \operatorname{ran}(R)=\{a,b,c\}.
          </md>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>
  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-properties-relations">
    <title>Properties of Relations</title>

    <p>
      Relations on a set can be classified according to several important
      properties.
    </p>

    <p>
      <ul>
        <li><p>Reflexive.</p></li>
        <li><p>Irreflexive.</p></li>
        <li><p>Symmetric.</p></li>
        <li><p>Antisymmetric.</p></li>
        <li><p>Asymmetric.</p></li>
        <li><p>Transitive.</p></li>
      </ul>
    </p>

    <p>These properties describe how the ordered pairs in a relation interact.</p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-reflexive-relations">
    <title>Reflexive Relations</title>

    <definition xml:id="def-reflexive-relation">
      <title>Reflexive Relation</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          A relation <m>R</m> on a set <m>A</m> is <em>reflexive</em> if
          every element is related to itself:
          <md>
            \forall a\in A,\;aRa.
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>The universal quantifier <m>\forall</m> is read <q>for every.</q></p>

    <p>
      For example, equality is reflexive because
      <md>
        a=a
      </md>
      for every <m>a</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      The relation <m>\leq</m> on <m>\R</m> is also reflexive because
      <md>
        a\leq a
      </md>
      for every real number <m>a</m>.
    </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-reflexive-pair-form">
    <title>Reflexivity in Ordered-Pair Form</title>

    <p>
      A relation <m>R</m> on <m>A</m> is reflexive exactly when
      <md>
        (a,a)\in R
      </md>
      for every <m>a\in A</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Thus, if
      <md>
        A=\{1,2,3\},
      </md>
      a reflexive relation must contain at least
      <md>
        (1,1),\qquad (2,2),\qquad (3,3).
      </md>
    </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-irreflexive-relations">
    <title>Irreflexive Relations</title>

    <definition xml:id="def-irreflexive-relation">
      <title>Irreflexive Relation</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          A relation <m>R</m> on <m>A</m> is <em>irreflexive</em> if no
          element is related to itself:
          <md>
            \forall a\in A,\;\neg(aRa).
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>The symbol <m>\neg</m> is the <em>negation symbol</em> and is read <q>not.</q></p>

    <p>
      The strict inequality <m>&lt;</m> is irreflexive because
      <md>
        a&lt;a
      </md>
      is false for every real number <m>a</m>.
    </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-reflexive-vs-irreflexive">
    <title>Reflexive versus Irreflexive</title>

    <p>
      Reflexive and irreflexive are stronger conditions than simply
      <q>not reflexive.</q>
    </p>

    <p>A relation that is not reflexive need not be irreflexive.</p>

    <p>
      For example, on
      <md>
        A=\{1,2\},
      </md>
      consider
      <md>
        R=\{(1,1)\}.
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      This relation is not reflexive because <m>(2,2)\notin R</m>.
      It is not irreflexive either because <m>(1,1)\in R</m>.
    </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-symmetric-relations">
    <title>Symmetric Relations</title>

    <definition xml:id="def-symmetric-relation">
      <title>Symmetric Relation</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          A relation <m>R</m> on <m>A</m> is <em>symmetric</em> if
          <md>
            aRb\Longrightarrow bRa.
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>The single arrow <m>\Longrightarrow</m> is read <q>implies.</q></p>

    <p>
      Thus symmetry means that whenever one direction occurs, the reverse
      direction also occurs.
    </p>

    <p>
      For example, equality is symmetric:
      <md>
        a=b\Longrightarrow b=a.
      </md>
    </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-symmetric-pair-form">
    <title>Symmetry in Ordered Pairs</title>

    <p>
      A relation <m>R</m> is symmetric if
      <md>
        (a,b)\in R\Longrightarrow(b,a)\in R.
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      Thus if a symmetric relation contains
      <md>
        (2,5),
      </md>
      it must also contain
      <md>
        (5,2).
      </md>
    </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-antisymmetric-relations">
    <title>Antisymmetric Relations</title>

    <definition xml:id="def-antisymmetric-relation">
      <title>Antisymmetric Relation</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          A relation <m>R</m> on <m>A</m> is <em>antisymmetric</em> if
          <md>
            aRb\text{ and }bRa\Longrightarrow a=b.
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>
      Antisymmetry allows two-way relationships only when the two elements
      are the same.
    </p>

    <p>
      The relation <m>\leq</m> is antisymmetric because
      <md>
        a\leq b\text{ and }b\leq a\Longrightarrow a=b.
      </md>
    </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-antisymmetric-not-nonsymmetric">
    <title>Antisymmetric Does Not Mean Not Symmetric</title>

    <p>This distinction is important.</p>

    <p>A relation can be both symmetric and antisymmetric.</p>

    <p>
      For example, equality satisfies both. Indeed,
      <md>
        a=b\Longrightarrow b=a,
      </md>
      so equality is symmetric.
    </p>

    <p>
      Also,
      <md>
        a=b\text{ and }b=a\Longrightarrow a=b,
      </md>
      so it is antisymmetric.
    </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-asymmetric-relations">
    <title>Asymmetric Relations</title>

    <definition xml:id="def-asymmetric-relation">
      <title>Asymmetric Relation</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          A relation <m>R</m> is <em>asymmetric</em> if
          <md>
            aRb\Longrightarrow\neg(bRa).
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>If one direction holds, the reverse direction must fail.</p>

    <p>
      The strict order <m>&lt;</m> is asymmetric:
      <md>
        a&lt;b\Longrightarrow\neg(b&lt;a).
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>Every asymmetric relation is irreflexive.</p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-transitive-relations">
    <title>Transitive Relations</title>

    <definition xml:id="def-transitive-relation">
      <title>Transitive Relation</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          A relation <m>R</m> on <m>A</m> is <em>transitive</em> if
          <md>
            aRb\text{ and }bRc\Longrightarrow aRc.
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>
      For example, <m>&lt;</m> on <m>\R</m> is transitive:
      <md>
        a&lt;b\text{ and }b&lt;c\Longrightarrow a&lt;c.
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      Equality is also transitive:
      <md>
        a=b\text{ and }b=c\Longrightarrow a=c.
      </md>
    </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-nontransitive-example">
    <title>A Nontransitive Relation</title>

    <p>Suppose a relation on people means <q>has met.</q></p>

    <p>
      If Alice has met Ben and Ben has met Carlos, it does not follow that
      Alice has met Carlos.
    </p>

    <p>Therefore this relation is generally not transitive.</p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-relation-properties-summary">
    <title>Summary of Common Relation Properties</title>

    <table xml:id="table-relation-properties">
      <title>Common Relation Properties</title>
      <tabular>
        <row>
          <cell><em>Property</em></cell>
          <cell><em>Condition</em></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>Reflexive</cell>
          <cell><m>aRa</m> for every <m>a</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>Irreflexive</cell>
          <cell><m>\neg(aRa)</m> for every <m>a</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>Symmetric</cell>
          <cell><m>aRb\Rightarrow bRa</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>Antisymmetric</cell>
          <cell><m>aRb</m> and <m>bRa\Rightarrow a=b</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>Asymmetric</cell>
          <cell><m>aRb\Rightarrow\neg(bRa)</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>Transitive</cell>
          <cell><m>aRb</m> and <m>bRc\Rightarrow aRc</m></cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </table>
  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-worked-classify-finite-relation">
    <title>Worked Example: Classifying a Finite Relation</title>

    <example xml:id="ex-classify-finite-relation">
      <title>Classify a Relation</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let
          <md>
            A=\{1,2,3\}
          </md>
          and
          <md>
            R=\{(1,1),(2,2),(3,3),(1,2),(2,1)\}.
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <p>
          <em>Reflexive:</em> Yes. The pairs <m>(1,1),(2,2),(3,3)</m>
          are all in <m>R</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <em>Symmetric:</em> Yes. Both <m>(1,2)</m> and <m>(2,1)</m>
          occur.
        </p>
        <p>
          <em>Antisymmetric:</em> No. We have <m>1R2</m> and <m>2R1</m>,
          but <m>1\neq2</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <em>Transitive:</em> Yes. All required chained pairs are present.
        </p>
        <p>
          Therefore <m>R</m> is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive, but
          not antisymmetric.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>
  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-equivalence-relations">
    <title>Equivalence Relations</title>

    <definition xml:id="def-equivalence-relation">
      <title>Equivalence Relation</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          An <em>equivalence relation</em> is a relation that is:
          <ul>
            <li><p>reflexive;</p></li>
            <li><p>symmetric;</p></li>
            <li><p>transitive.</p></li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>
      Equivalence relations formalize the idea that two objects should be
      regarded as <q>the same</q> with respect to some chosen property.
    </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-equality-equivalence">
    <title>Equality as an Equivalence Relation</title>

    <p>Equality is the most familiar equivalence relation.</p>

    <p>
      It is reflexive:
      <md>
        a=a.
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      It is symmetric:
      <md>
        a=b\Longrightarrow b=a.
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      It is transitive:
      <md>
        a=b\text{ and }b=c\Longrightarrow a=c.
      </md>
    </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-same-parity-relation">
    <title>Same-Parity Relation</title>

    <p>
      Define a relation <m>R</m> on <m>\Z</m> by
      <md>
        aRb\Longleftrightarrow a-b\text{ is even}.
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>This means <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> have the same parity.</p>

    <p>
      For example,
      <md>
        3R7
      </md>
      because
      <md>
        3-7=-4
      </md>
      is even.
    </p>

    <p>
      But
      <md>
        (3,8)\notin R
      </md>
      because
      <md>
        3-8=-5
      </md>
      is odd.
    </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-worked-equivalence-relation">
    <title>Worked Example: Proving an Equivalence Relation</title>

    <example xml:id="ex-same-parity-equivalence">
      <title>Show Same Parity Is an Equivalence Relation</title>

      <statement>
        <p>
          Define
          <md>
            aRb\Longleftrightarrow a-b\text{ is even}.
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <p>
          <em>Reflexive:</em>
          <m>a-a=0</m>, and <m>0</m> is even.
        </p>

        <p>
          <em>Symmetric:</em>
          If <m>a-b=2k</m>, then <m>b-a=-2k</m>, which is even.
        </p>

        <p>
          <em>Transitive:</em>
          If <m>a-b=2m</m> and <m>b-c=2n</m>, then
          <md>
            a-c=2(m+n).
          </md>
        </p>

        <p>
          The same-parity relation is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive;
          therefore it is an equivalence relation.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>
  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-equivalence-classes">
    <title>Equivalence Classes</title>

    <p>
      If <m>R</m> is an equivalence relation on <m>A</m>, each element
      determines an equivalence class.
    </p>

    <definition xml:id="def-equivalence-class">
      <title>Equivalence Class</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          The <em>equivalence class</em> of <m>a\in A</m> is
          <md>
            [a]=\{x\in A:xRa\}.
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>The notation <m>[a]</m> is read <q>the equivalence class of <m>a</m>.</q></p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-parity-equivalence-classes">
    <title>Equivalence Classes for Same Parity</title>

    <p>
      For the same-parity relation on <m>\Z</m>, there are exactly two
      equivalence classes.
    </p>

    <p>
      The class of <m>0</m> is
      <md>
        [0]=\{\ldots,-4,-2,0,2,4,\ldots\}.
      </md>
      This is the set of even integers.
    </p>

    <p>
      The class of <m>1</m> is
      <md>
        [1]=\{\ldots,-3,-1,1,3,5,\ldots\}.
      </md>
      This is the set of odd integers.
    </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-equal-equivalence-classes">
    <title>Equivalent Elements Have the Same Class</title>

    <p>
      If
      <md>
        aRb,
      </md>
      then
      <md>
        [a]=[b].
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      If <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> are not related, their equivalence classes
      are disjoint.
    </p>

    <p>
      Thus equivalence classes divide the original set into nonoverlapping
      pieces.
    </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-relations-partitions">
    <title>Partitions</title>

    <p>
      Recall that a partition divides a set into nonempty, pairwise disjoint
      subsets whose union is the original set.
    </p>

    <p>Every equivalence relation determines a partition.</p>

    <p>Its equivalence classes form the pieces of that partition.</p>

    <p>
      Conversely, every partition determines an equivalence relation by
      declaring two elements equivalent when they belong to the same piece.
    </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-congruence-mod-n">
    <title>Congruence Modulo <m>n</m></title>

    <p>
      One of the most important equivalence relations in number theory is
      congruence modulo <m>n</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      For integers <m>a,b</m> and positive integer <m>n</m>,
      <md>
        a\equiv b\pmod n
      </md>
      means that
      <md>
        n\mid(a-b).
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      The symbol <m>\equiv</m> is called the <em>congruence symbol</em> in
      this context and is read <q>is congruent to.</q>
    </p>

    <p>The notation <m>\pmod n</m> is read <q>modulo <m>n</m>.</q></p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-divisibility-symbol-relations">
    <title>The Divisibility Symbol</title>

    <p>
      The vertical bar in
      <md>
        n\mid m
      </md>
      is read <q><m>n</m> divides <m>m</m>.</q>
    </p>

    <p>
      It means there exists an integer <m>k</m> such that
      <md>
        m=nk.
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      Thus,
      <md>
        7\equiv2\pmod5
      </md>
      because
      <md>
        5\mid(7-2).
      </md>
    </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-order-relations">
    <title>Order Relations</title>

    <p>Relations can also describe order rather than equivalence.</p>

    <p>
      Examples include
      <md>
        \leq,\qquad \geq,\qquad \subseteq,\qquad \mid.
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>These relations compare objects according to some structure.</p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-partial-orders">
    <title>Partial Orders</title>

    <definition xml:id="def-partial-order">
      <title>Partial Order</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          A <em>partial order</em> is a relation that is:
          <ul>
            <li><p>reflexive;</p></li>
            <li><p>antisymmetric;</p></li>
            <li><p>transitive.</p></li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>
      A set together with a partial order is called a
      <em>partially ordered set</em>, or <em>poset</em>.
    </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-subset-partial-order">
    <title>Subset Inclusion as a Partial Order</title>

    <p>
      For a collection of sets, the relation
      <md>
        \subseteq
      </md>
      is a partial order.
    </p>

    <p>
      It is reflexive:
      <md>
        A\subseteq A.
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      It is antisymmetric:
      <md>
        A\subseteq B\text{ and }B\subseteq A\Longrightarrow A=B.
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      It is transitive:
      <md>
        A\subseteq B\text{ and }B\subseteq C
        \Longrightarrow
        A\subseteq C.
      </md>
    </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-comparability">
    <title>Comparability</title>

    <p>
      In a partially ordered set, two elements <m>a,b</m> are
      <em>comparable</em> if
      <md>
        aRb\qquad\text{or}\qquad bRa.
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>Not every pair in a partial order must be comparable.</p>

    <p>
      For example, under subset inclusion,
      <md>
        \{1\}
      </md>
      and
      <md>
        \{2\}
      </md>
      are not comparable because neither is a subset of the other.
    </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-total-orders">
    <title>Total Orders</title>

    <definition xml:id="def-total-order">
      <title>Total Order</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          A <em>total order</em> is a partial order in which every pair of
          elements is comparable.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>
      The ordinary order <m>\leq</m> on <m>\R</m> is a total order because
      for any real numbers <m>a,b</m>,
      <md>
        a\leq b\qquad\text{or}\qquad b\leq a.
      </md>
    </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-strict-orders">
    <title>Strict Orders</title>

    <p>
      The strict relation
      <md>
        &lt;
      </md>
      differs from <m>\leq</m>.
    </p>

    <p>It is irreflexive rather than reflexive.</p>

    <p>It is asymmetric and transitive.</p>

    <p>
      Strict and non-strict order relations often appear together:
      <md>
        a&lt;b
      </md>
      versus
      <md>
        a\leq b.
      </md>
    </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-inverse-relations">
    <title>Inverse Relations</title>

    <p>
      The notation
      <md>
        R^{-1}
      </md>
      is read <q><m>R</m> inverse.</q>
    </p>

    <definition xml:id="def-inverse-relation">
      <title>Inverse Relation</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          If
          <md>
            R\subseteq A\times B,
          </md>
          then the inverse relation is
          <md>
            R^{-1}=\{(b,a):(a,b)\in R\}.
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>The inverse relation reverses every ordered pair.</p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-worked-inverse-relation">
    <title>Worked Example: Inverse Relation</title>

    <example xml:id="ex-inverse-relation">
      <title>Find an Inverse Relation</title>

      <statement>
        <p>
          Let
          <md>
            R=\{(1,a),(2,b),(3,a)\}.
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <p>Reverse <m>(1,a)</m> to obtain <m>(a,1)</m>.</p>
        <p>Reverse <m>(2,b)</m> to obtain <m>(b,2)</m>.</p>
        <p>Reverse <m>(3,a)</m> to obtain <m>(a,3)</m>.</p>

        <p>
          Therefore,
          <md>
            R^{-1}=\{(a,1),(b,2),(a,3)\}.
          </md>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>
  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-composition-relations">
    <title>Composition of Relations</title>

    <p>Relations can be chained together.</p>

    <p>
      Suppose
      <md>
        R\subseteq A\times B
      </md>
      and
      <md>
        S\subseteq B\times C.
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      The symbol <m>\circ</m> is called the <em>composition symbol</em>
      and is read <q>composed with.</q>
    </p>

    <definition xml:id="def-composition-relations">
      <title>Composition of Relations</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          The composition of <m>S</m> with <m>R</m> is
          <md>
            S\circ R
            =
            \{(a,c):\exists b\in B,\ (a,b)\in R
            \text{ and }(b,c)\in S\}.
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>
      Thus, <m>a</m> is connected to <m>c</m> whenever there is an
      intermediate element <m>b</m> linking them.
    </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-worked-relation-composition">
    <title>Worked Example: Composition</title>

    <example xml:id="ex-relation-composition">
      <title>Compose Two Relations</title>

      <statement>
        <p>
          Let
          <md>
            R=\{(1,a),(2,b),(3,a)\}
          </md>
          and
          <md>
            S=\{(a,x),(a,y),(b,z)\}.
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <p>
          <m>1</m> connects to <m>a</m>, and <m>a</m> connects to
          <m>x,y</m>, giving <m>(1,x),(1,y)</m>.
        </p>

        <p>
          <m>2</m> connects to <m>b</m>, and <m>b</m> connects to
          <m>z</m>, giving <m>(2,z)</m>.
        </p>

        <p>
          <m>3</m> connects to <m>a</m>, and <m>a</m> connects to
          <m>x,y</m>, giving <m>(3,x),(3,y)</m>.
        </p>

        <p>
          Therefore,
          <md>
            S\circ R
            =
            \{(1,x),(1,y),(2,z),(3,x),(3,y)\}.
          </md>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>
  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-relations-functions-bridge">
    <title>Relations and Functions</title>

    <p>Every function can be represented as a relation.</p>

    <p>However, not every relation is a function.</p>

    <p>
      For a relation
      <md>
        R\subseteq A\times B
      </md>
      to represent a function from <m>A</m> to <m>B</m>, every element of
      <m>A</m> must be associated with exactly one element of <m>B</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Thus a function is a relation satisfying an additional uniqueness
      requirement.
    </p>

    <p>Functions are developed formally in <xref ref="sec-functions"/>.</p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-common-relation-errors">
    <title>Common Relation Errors</title>

    <subsubsection xml:id="subsec-relation-error-symmetric-antisymmetric">
      <title>Confusing Symmetric and Antisymmetric</title>

      <p>
        A symmetric relation satisfies
        <md>
          aRb\Longrightarrow bRa.
        </md>
      </p>

      <p>
        An antisymmetric relation satisfies
        <md>
          aRb\text{ and }bRa\Longrightarrow a=b.
        </md>
      </p>

      <p>These definitions are not opposites.</p>
    </subsubsection>


    <subsubsection xml:id="subsec-relation-error-one-example">
      <title>Checking Only One Example</title>

      <p>
        To establish that a relation is reflexive, symmetric, or transitive,
        checking a few examples is not enough.
      </p>

      <p>The condition must hold for all required elements.</p>

      <p>
        A single counterexample, however, is enough to show that a universal
        property fails.
      </p>
    </subsubsection>


    <subsubsection xml:id="subsec-relation-error-transitivity">
      <title>Misreading Transitivity</title>

      <p>
        Transitivity requires a matching middle element:
        <md>
          aRb,\qquad bRc.
        </md>
      </p>

      <p>
        Only then does transitivity require
        <md>
          aRc.
        </md>
      </p>

      <p>Unrelated pairs do not create a transitivity requirement.</p>
    </subsubsection>


    <subsubsection xml:id="subsec-relation-error-function">
      <title>Assuming Every Relation Is a Function</title>

      <p>A relation may associate one input with several outputs.</p>

      <p>
        For example,
        <md>
          R=\{(1,a),(1,b)\}
        </md>
        is a valid relation but does not define a function with input
        <m>1</m>, because the same input is associated with two different
        outputs.
      </p>
    </subsubsection>
  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-relations-worked-examples">
    <title>Worked Examples</title>

    <example xml:id="ex-test-reflexivity">
      <title>Test Reflexivity</title>

      <statement>
        <p>
          Let
          <md>
            A=\{1,2,3\}
          </md>
          and
          <md>
            R=\{(1,1),(2,2),(1,3),(3,1)\}.
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <p><m>(1,1)\in R</m>.</p>
        <p><m>(2,2)\in R</m>.</p>
        <p><m>(3,3)\notin R</m>.</p>
        <p>Therefore <m>R</m> is not reflexive.</p>
      </solution>
    </example>


    <example xml:id="ex-test-symmetry">
      <title>Test Symmetry</title>

      <statement>
        <p>
          Let
          <md>
            R=\{(1,2),(2,1),(2,3)\}.
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <p>
          The reverse of <m>(1,2)</m>, namely <m>(2,1)</m>, is present.
        </p>
        <p>
          The reverse of <m>(2,3)</m>, namely <m>(3,2)</m>, is absent.
        </p>
        <p>Therefore <m>R</m> is not symmetric.</p>
      </solution>
    </example>


    <example xml:id="ex-test-partial-order">
      <title>Test a Partial Order</title>

      <statement>
        <p>
          Consider divisibility on the positive integers:
          <md>
            aRb\Longleftrightarrow a\mid b.
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <p>
          <em>Reflexive:</em> <m>a\mid a</m> because <m>a=a(1)</m>.
        </p>

        <p>
          <em>Antisymmetric:</em> <m>a\mid b</m> and <m>b\mid a</m>
          imply <m>a=b</m> for positive integers.
        </p>

        <p>
          <em>Transitive:</em> <m>a\mid b</m> and <m>b\mid c</m>
          imply <m>a\mid c</m>.
        </p>

        <p>Divisibility is a partial order on the positive integers.</p>
      </solution>
    </example>
  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-relations-exercises">
    <title>Exercises</title>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-relations-01">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let
          <md>
            A=\{1,2,3\}
          </md>
          and
          <md>
            R=\{(1,2),(2,3),(3,1)\}.
          </md>
          Find <m>\operatorname{dom}(R)</m> and
          <m>\operatorname{ran}(R)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-relations-02">
      <statement>
        <p>
          For the relation
          <md>
            R=\{(1,1),(2,2),(3,3)\}
          </md>
          on <m>\{1,2,3\}</m>, determine whether <m>R</m> is reflexive,
          symmetric, antisymmetric, and transitive.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-relations-03">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Determine whether the relation <m>&lt;</m> on <m>\R</m> is
          reflexive, irreflexive, symmetric, antisymmetric, asymmetric, and
          transitive.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-relations-04">
      <statement>
        <p>Determine whether equality on <m>\Z</m> is an equivalence relation.</p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-relations-05">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let
          <md>
            aRb\Longleftrightarrow a-b\text{ is divisible by }3
          </md>
          on <m>\Z</m>. Show that <m>R</m> is an equivalence relation.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-relations-06">
      <statement>
        <p>Find the equivalence classes for congruence modulo <m>3</m>.</p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-relations-07">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let
          <md>
            R=\{(1,a),(2,b),(2,c)\}.
          </md>
          Find <m>R^{-1}</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-relations-08">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let
          <md>
            R=\{(1,a),(2,b)\},
            \qquad
            S=\{(a,x),(b,y),(b,z)\}.
          </md>
          Find <m>S\circ R</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-relations-09">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Determine whether <m>\subseteq</m> is a partial order on
          <m>\mathcal{P}(A)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-relations-10">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Give an example of a relation that is neither reflexive nor
          irreflexive.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-relations-11">
      <statement>
        <p>Give an example of a relation that is symmetric but not transitive.</p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-relations-12">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Give an example of a relation that is antisymmetric but not symmetric.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-relations-13">
      <statement>
        <p>Explain why every asymmetric relation is irreflexive.</p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-relations-14">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Explain why an equivalence relation determines a partition of its
          underlying set.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-relations-15">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Prove that if <m>R</m> is an equivalence relation and <m>aRb</m>,
          then
          <md>
            [a]=[b].
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-relations-16">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Prove that distinct equivalence classes of an equivalence relation
          are disjoint.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-relations-17">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Suppose <m>R</m> and <m>S</m> are relations for which the
          compositions are defined. Investigate whether
          <md>
            (S\circ R)^{-1}=R^{-1}\circ S^{-1}.
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-relations-connections">
    <title>Section Connections</title>

    <assemblage>
      <title>Connections</title>

      <p>
        Relations extend the Cartesian-product ideas developed in
        <xref ref="sec-elementary-set-theory"/>.
        Functions in <xref ref="sec-functions"/> are special relations with a
        unique-output condition. Equivalence relations later produce quotient
        structures in algebra and number theory, while partial orders lead to
        order theory and lattice theory. Relation composition also anticipates
        function composition, matrix multiplication, graph paths, and
        categorical composition.
      </p>
    </assemblage>
  </subsection>

</section>